sections.body:
In Berlin your licence file lands at the LABO, the city's licensing authority (Fahrerlaubnisbehörde). Three things must be in that file before you may sit an exam:

- an **eyesight test** (Sehtest) — you pass with visual acuity of at least **0.7/0.7**, with glasses or contact lenses if you need them; fail it and you need a full ophthalmologist's report;
- a **first-aid course** (Erste-Hilfe-Kurs) of at least **nine units of 45 minutes**;
- a **passport photo, ID and application** — plus, for foreign residents, proof of registration (Anmeldung).

The theory exam for class B is **30 questions**: 20 on the basic material (Grundstoff) and 10 on class-specific material (Zusatzstoff). Wrong answers cost error points (Fehlerpunkte), and you may collect at most **10**. There is one nasty extra rule: get **two 5-point questions wrong** and you fail, even though that is exactly 10 points. Once you pass, you have **12 months** to pass the practical test (praktische Prüfung) before the theory expires.

If glasses are what got you through the Sehtest, a code is entered in your licence — and driving without them from then on is a violation, not a style choice.
